// @run
// @log a
// @log 7
// @log 3
// B9 CLOSED — and it took TWO HALVES, which is why it stayed open for four slices.
//
// A generic whose return union COLLAPSES under its binding, read into an INFERRED binding,
// was check-clean INVALID WASM: `vl check --codegen` rc 0 and `vl run` "expected
// (ref null $type), found (ref $type)". The global was declared over the shared VALUE-UNION
// BOX while the instance returns a bare ref.
//
// HALF ONE, the CHECKER (`substTyDeep`, shipped separately): the substitution walk rebuilt
// `T | string` at `T := string` as a two-member union of identical members, where the
// annotation path folds it to plain `string`. Fixing that made the checker right.
//
// HALF TWO, the EMITTER (`monoSubstAnn`, here): the rejoin is a blind concat, so the
// instance's return NAME was still the spelling `string|string` — a name no source program
// can produce and no interner has seen. Every consumer reading the name still saw a union.
// It now flattens each substituted atom (one may itself be a union, `T := string | null`),
// dedupes by FIRST OCCURRENCE so a union whose arms do not collapse re-renders byte-
// identically and is never a new key, and returns the BARE atom when one survives.
//
// NEITHER HALF WORKS ALONE, and the emitter half was measured at **0 outcomes moved** over a
// 16-cell grid when it was tried by itself — correctly measured and wrongly concluded. It
// was not useless; it was incomplete, and the B9 note that filed it as "not the cause" is
// superseded by this file. A change that moves nothing may be one half of a pair.
//
// Held fixed: the arms that do NOT collapse must keep their union box, which is the third
// line below.
function f<T>(v: T): T | string {
  v
}

function g<T>(v: T): T | i32 {
  v
}

// COLLAPSING, inferred binding — the defect.
const a = f("a")
print(a)

const b = g(7)
if b is i32 { print(b) }

// The control: arms that stay DISTINCT keep the union.
const c = f(3)
if c is i32 { print(c) }
